\section{Introduction and Operator-Algebraic Preliminaries}

Let $\mathcal{A} = \mathcal{A}_0 \oplus \mathcal{A}_1$ be a unital, separable, associative $\mathbb{Z}_2$-graded $C^*$-algebra over $\C$, equipped with a canonical grading $^*$-automorphism $\Gamma : \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{A}$ satisfying $\Gamma^2 = \id_{\mathcal{A}}$, $\Gamma|_{\mathcal{A}_0} = +\id_{\mathcal{A}_0}$, and $\Gamma|_{\mathcal{A}_1} = -\id_{\mathcal{A}_1}$. Let $\mathcal{S}(\mathcal{A})$ denote the weak-$^*$ compact convex state space of normalized positive linear functionals on $\mathcal{A}$, and let $\mathcal{A}^* = \mathcal{A}_0^* \oplus \mathcal{A}_1^*$ be the Banach dual decomposition into even and odd continuous linear functionals satisfying $\varphi \circ \Gamma = \pm \varphi$.

A central question in noncommutative geometry and spectral analysis is the behavior of homogeneous functional decompositions and graded traces under singular parameter limits where faithful representations degenerate into boundary quotients. In this paper, we formalize this phenomenon through conjugate-linear grading-reversing involutions and establish sharp spectral lower bounds and supertrace invariants on associated graded Hilbert--Fock modules.

