\textbf{Step 3 (Preservation of $\mathrm{SO}^+(1, 3)$ covariance).} Because the $\Z_2$-holonomy twist acts through the central kernel $\Ker(\Phi) = \{I_2, -I_2\}$ of the universal cover $\Phi : \mathrm{SL}(2, \C) \twoheadrightarrow \mathrm{SO}^+(1, 3)$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:sl2c-final}), which commutes with every $A \in \mathrm{SL}(2, \C)$, the mass gap $\Delta m_{\mathrm{YM}}$ is a central Casimir invariant descending to an exact Lorentz-invariant scalar under $\mathrm{SO}^+(1, 3)$.
\end{proof}

\section{Concluding Remarks}

We have established a self-contained operator-algebraic and geometric framework in which $\Z_2$-graded asymptotic dualities ($0 \leftrightarrow 1$), horizon boundary regimes, and low-energy gauge spectral gaps are unified under the universal spinorial double cover $\mathrm{SL}(2, \C)/\Z_2 \cong \mathrm{SO}^+(1, 3)$ and flat $\Z_2$-holonomy twists on compactified circular fibers $S^1_\pi$. Together, these mechanisms generate strictly positive Lorentz-invariant spectral gaps without ad hoc scalar Higgs potentials or indefinite-metric Faddeev--Popov ghosts, while preserving unitary trace conservation and non-vanishing spectral lower bounds for stationary Dirac fields.

